Theorem 5.1, packaged as a countably algebraic lattice on the fixed points of a closure. The countable compact basis is the family of fixed points generated by the finite sets e n. Each basis element is compact, every compact fixed point arises as one of them, and every fixed point is the supremum of the compact fixed points below it. The density proof identifies those compact elements with the set of basis points contained in the given fixed point, then quotes the supremum decomposition.
